      "text" : "Seek three diamond tricks to supplement two spades, two hearts and two clubs. Lead D3 and normally duck in dummy with D5, preserving both diamond honors. Then play toward KQ to drive out the ace and establish the long cards. A 3–3 diamond break supplies three tricks at the cost of two concessions, subject to surviving defensive counterplay. Some doubleton-ace holdings also succeed, but not all: an ace duck combined with adverse intermediate cards can restrict diamonds to two tricks. Do not assume nine tricks merely because the ace falls on round two. Use SQ against a spade continuation and preserve club entries, particularly CA for access to established diamonds; cash CK before crossing when needed. The main danger is two diamond losers followed by three defensive spade tricks. Track the diamond spots and adjust honor plays to actual developments. If diamonds cannot supply three tricks, retain club flexibility for a possible extra trick through CT, or reassess hearts as the defenders reveal their holdings. Cash established winners once nine tricks are available.",
